<PageLayout title="Exploring Genomes. Finding Life." kicker="MANIFESTO // why this laboratory exists" lead="Homogmos Lab is built around a simple idea: genomes are among the richest historical archives ever produced by life itself.">
<section><div class="manifesto">
<p>For centuries, biology explored life by observing organisms. Then cells. Then molecules.</p>
<p>Today, we can observe something else.</p><p class="strike">We can observe history.</p>
<p>Every genome is more than a sequence of nucleotides. It is a historical document: a record shaped by migrations, adaptation, extinction, ancient populations, failed experiments and successful innovations.</p>
<p>Most of those histories remain unread. Not because they disappeared, but because we still lack many of the questions, methods and tools needed to recover them.</p>
<p>Homogmos Lab exists to explore those archives through evolutionary genomics, computational biology, paleogenomics, scientific software and open scientific tools.</p>
<p>Some projects may become publications. Others may become software, interactive atlases, datasets, field notes or ideas that lead to entirely different questions.</p>
<p>Science is rarely a straight path. Failed hypotheses, unexpected results and rewritten questions are not detours from discovery. They are part of discovery.</p>
<p>For that reason, Homogmos Lab is not only a place to present finished results. It is a place to document exploration, uncertainty, methods, tools and the questions that remain.</p>
<p class="strike">Every genome tells a story.<br/>We are here to learn how to read it.</p>
<p class="small">HOMOGMOS LAB // MANIFESTO v1.0</p>
</div></section>
</PageLayout>